.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:11:[ADR-0114](0114-scoring-unification.md) R1.7 placed `user_feedback_weight` as a canonical scoring factor and referenced a `FeedbackAction` enum with placeholder values `StillTrue | Outdated | WrongSource`. [ADR-0113](0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md) §6 declared the agent trust-ledger updates as "User accepts an agent claim → α += 1 / User contradicts → β += 1" without typing accept vs. contradict.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:13:The v1.4.0 Claim Feedback and Prompt Granularity Review identified that a 5-point accuracy scale or yes/no boolean cannot drive distinct trust, provenance, and repair behavior. Different user intents require different DB writes:
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:15:- "Wrong account" — subject-fit failure; suppress on this entity; do not punish source.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:17:- "Wrong source" — attribution failure; downweight source for this claim_type; keep claim open.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:35:    ///   via the freshness factor, not the source factor; α += 0.5 on the agent
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:40:    /// → claim transitions to `withdrawn`; tombstone written if no remaining
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:41:    ///   sources support an alternate value; β += 1.0 on agent ledger; one-shot
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:42:    ///   modest source downweight (-0.05) — single mistakes may be misclassification.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:49:    /// → claim suppressed on the asserted subject (per-subject tombstone);
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:52:    ///   source reliability NOT downweighted.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:54:    WrongSubject { corrected_to: Option<SubjectRef> },
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:56:    /// User says the cited source does not actually support the claim.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:57:    /// → source attribution downweighted (-0.2) on this source for this claim_type;
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:58:    ///   claim remains open if other sources clear the trust floor; transitions
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:59:    ///   to `withdrawn` if no remaining sources qualify; β += 0.2 on agent.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:60:    WrongSource { source_index: usize },
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:64:    ///   targeted repair job enqueued (per §7); if no corroboration found within
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:71:    ///   original claim transitions to `superseded`; source remains attributed
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:74:    NeedsNuance { corrected_text: String },
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:79:    ///   no source penalty; feeds ADR-0108 surface privacy policy.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:89:Nine variants, each mapping to a distinct triple of (claim state, source weight, agent ledger). No 5-point scale. No yes/no.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:91:### 2. `ClaimFeedback` row shape (closed)
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:94:pub struct ClaimFeedback {
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:95:    pub id: ClaimFeedbackId,
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:106:Stored in `claim_feedback` table. **Append-only.** The Trust Compiler reads the latest unconsumed rows on each scoring pass and writes `applied_at` after consuming. Re-application is idempotent: a row with `applied_at IS NOT NULL` is skipped.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:112:| 1 | Which feedback actions are first-class in v1.4.0 vs. v1.4.2? | All nine variants are first-class in the **enum and DB row** in v1.4.0 (DOS-294). Inline UI surfaces in DOS-8 expose six (`ConfirmCurrent`, `MarkOutdated`, `MarkFalse`, `WrongSubject`, `WrongSource`, `CannotVerify`) behind a low/medium-trust gate. The remaining three (`NeedsNuance`, `SurfaceInappropriate`, `NotRelevantHere`) ship behind an overflow menu in v1.4.2. | §1; UI work split between DOS-8 (v1.4.0) and v1.4.2 |
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:113:| 2 | Does `CannotVerify` affect trust score directly? | No direct delta. Triggers a `NeedsCorroboration` warning + repair-job enqueue. If no corroboration found within 14 days, the claim downweights via `lack-of-corroboration` in `scoring::factors`, not via user-rejection. | §1 `CannotVerify`; §7 |
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:117:| 6 | Which claim types are editable directly vs. structured-only? | `NeedsNuance` with free text is allowed for: account assertions, person role descriptions, project status text. Structured-only (no `NeedsNuance`; only typed corrections via `WrongSubject` / `WrongSource` / `MarkFalse`): stakeholder roles, commitment owners, renewal dates, health bands. The structured-only set is anything where the UI already provides a typed input — text edit there would defeat the typing. | Decided; enforced by `claim_type` metadata on the claim row (DOS-7) |
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:118:| 7 | Query/concurrency budget for targeted claim repair? | Per `CannotVerify`: one repair job enqueued, executes within 24 hours, capped at 1 LLM call + 1 retrieval batch (≤10 sources). Per entity: max 5 active repair jobs. Per workspace: max 50 active repair jobs. Excess queues with low priority. | §7; enforced via DOS-236 invalidation queue |
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:122:The Trust Compiler (DOS-5) consumes `ClaimFeedback` via the `user_feedback_weight` factor (per ADR-0114 R1.3, the factor takes pre-computed `age_days`, not a clock). Per-action deltas:
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:129:| `WrongSubject` | none | β += 0.3 | → tombstoned at this subject | unchanged | -0.3 on the inference method that produced this attachment |
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:130:| `WrongSource` | -0.20 on cited source for this claim_type | β += 0.2 | unchanged unless no sources remain qualified | unchanged | unchanged |
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:131:| `CannotVerify` | none | none | unchanged | unchanged | unchanged; triggers repair |
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:132:| `NeedsNuance` | none | α += 0.3 (refinement) or β += 0.3 (contradiction) by text-overlap heuristic ≥ 0.5 | original → `superseded`; new claim committed | refreshed on new claim | inherits |
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:136:These deltas are the spine. **Tuning happens in v1.4.1** against shadow data; the `agent_trust_ledger` and source-weight tables are designed to recompute, so retroactive tuning is safe.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:142:- ADR-0113 §5 tombstone behavior is now triggered by `WrongSubject` (per-subject tombstone) and `MarkFalse` (broad tombstone), not by an untyped UI gesture.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:143:- DOS-294 ships the `FeedbackAction` enum + `ClaimFeedback` row + service functions (`propose_feedback`, `apply_feedback_to_trust`).
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:144:- DOS-8 inline UI uses the typed enum directly. No more untyped `feedback_yes/no/wrong_source` handlers.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:148:`WrongSubject { corrected_to }` is the user-side complement to ADR-0105's `SubjectAttribution` substrate primitive. Behavior:
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:153:The inference method that produced the wrong attachment receives `subject_evidence` downweight `-0.3`. After repeated `WrongSubject` feedback against the same `InferenceMethod` for the same source class, the inference method gets globally suppressed for that source class (threshold: 5 instances within 30 days).
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:159:1. A `claim_repair_job` row is enqueued via DOS-236 invalidation queue with `priority = normal`.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:160:2. Job retrieves up to 10 candidate corroborating sources via Glean + local context.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:165:Budgets: 1 LLM call + 1 retrieval batch per job; max 5 active repair jobs per entity; max 50 per workspace. Excess queues at `priority = low`.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:169:- UI surfaces beyond DOS-8's inline six. Overflow menu (`NeedsNuance` editor, `SurfaceInappropriate`, `NotRelevantHere`), claim detail panel, lint feedback debt — v1.4.2.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:170:- Cross-claim feedback aggregation ("user often marks Glean Salesforce sources wrong" → systematic source downweight). Tracked separately for v1.5.0+.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:180:- Per-action behavior is specified before code lands. No ambiguity about whether `MarkFalse` punishes the source or the claim.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:181:- `WrongSubject` becomes a substrate-level operation pairing cleanly with ADR-0105 `SubjectAttribution`. Subject mistakes do not punish sources.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:188:- `NeedsNuance` text-overlap heuristic for "refinement vs. contradiction" is approximate. Acceptable in v1.4.0; can be replaced with a small classifier in v1.4.1.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:190:- Repair job queue depends on DOS-236 (durable invalidation), which is v1.4.1 scope. v1.4.0 ships the `claim_repair_job` row schema + enqueue stub; the worker runs in v1.4.1. `CannotVerify` feedback collected in v1.4.0 is queued and processed once the worker lands.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:194:- No new tables beyond `claim_feedback` and `claim_repair_job` (skeleton). ADR-0113's existing tables absorb the structural updates.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:199:- [ADR-0105: Provenance as First-Class Output](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) — `SubjectAttribution` amendment supplies `SubjectRef` consumed by `WrongSubject`.
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:200:- [ADR-0113: Human and Agent Analysis as First-Class Claim Sources](0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md) — agent-ledger updates typed via this ADR (§6 amended by §4 here).
.docs/decisions/0123-typed-claim-feedback-semantics.md:203:- DOS-294 — implementation issue; ships `FeedbackAction` + `ClaimFeedback` + service functions.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:6:**Extends:** [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md), [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md), [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md), [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md), [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md)
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:8:**Consumed by:** [DOS-7](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-7) (`intelligence_claims.actor` column, tombstones), [DOS-241](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-241) (enrichment refactor design)
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:15:2. [DOS-7](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-7) elevates `intelligence_claims` to a first-class table with per-claim identity and append-only assertion history.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:22:This ADR specifies the actor taxonomy, the propose/commit boundary, the contradiction-handling rules, the agent trust ledger, and the deduplication policy that collectively make humans and agents first-class, equal-structured claim sources. The substrate pieces needed in v1.4.0 are limited: an `actor` column and a `claim_state` column on `intelligence_claims`, plus the propose/commit state machine. The user-facing surfaces (Analysis Inbox, agent trust dashboard, contradiction resolution UI) ship in v1.5.0+ and are out of scope for v1.4.0.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:33:| `user_removal` | `user_removal` | `user_removal` | User removed a value (tombstone) |
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:37:| `external:<source>` | `external:salesforce`, `external:glean` | | Mirrored from an external system of record; the external system is the authoritative asserter |
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:41:- An actor value is append-only to the catalog; once shipped, its shape does not change.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:53:| `retracted` | Claim was withdrawn (tombstone, source revocation, contradiction resolution). | Included in history; tombstones consulted by writers; otherwise excluded from default reads. |
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:66:strategy = "immediate"                 # tombstones commit immediately
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:75:strategy = "immediate"                 # external source of record is authoritative
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:81:corroboration_threshold = 2            # 2 independent sources agreeing
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:88:- The claim has `corroboration_threshold` or more independent sources producing identical or compatible assertions at the same `field_path` within the corroboration window (default 7 days).
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:97:- **Maintenance abilities** consume `ClaimProposal` + `AbilityContext` and apply the commit policy. The write itself happens inside a service function (`services/claims.rs::commit_claim` or `::propose_claim`) that honors [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) mode gates and [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md) safety constraints.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:105:A tombstone is a claim with:
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:107:- `actor = 'user'` (or `user_removal` for the specific tombstone variant when the user explicitly cleared a field)
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:110:- `claim_state = 'committed'` (tombstones commit immediately per §3)
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:112:Tombstones participate in supersede semantics: when a user removes a field value, a tombstone claim is written and supersedes any prior committed claim at the same `(entity_id, claim_type, field_path)`.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:114:**Writers must consult tombstones before proposing — PRE-GATE CHECK (clarified 2026-04-20 per outside voice finding #3).** The tombstone check is **not** part of the commit policy gate. It is a **hard pre-gate rejection** that runs inside `services::claims::propose_claim` before any commit policy evaluation. Order of operations:
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:116:1. Tombstone check (this §5) — reject any actor targeting a tombstoned `field_path` within the tombstone window, regardless of commit policy config.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:120:This order matters because it means **the ghost-resurrection fix is live in v1.4.0 even when the commit policy config is `immediate` for all actor classes** (which R1.9 specifies for v1.4.0). The tombstone protection does not depend on the gate activating; it's a structural pre-check. When v1.4.1 flips agent policy to `gated`, the tombstone check still runs first.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:122:An agent wishing to override a tombstone must produce a higher-threshold corroboration (e.g., three independent sources within 7 days) and surface the override as a contradiction (§7) rather than a silent commit.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:147:- Corroboration by an independent source within the window → `alpha += 0.5`.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:148:- Contradiction by an independent source → `beta += 0.5`.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:193:- **Match and within window** → do not create a new row; increment `corroboration_count` on the existing row; append the new `source_ref` to its corroboration list; update `trust_computed_at`.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:204:- A new field on `Provenance`: `proposal_context: Option<ProposalContext>` carries the trust-gate outcome (`auto_commit | corroboration_commit | proposed | rejected_by_tombstone`) and the gate inputs at commit time. Populated only for Maintenance ability calls that write claims.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:209:[ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md)'s `DataSource` enum is **not** extended with new variants for actors. `DataSource` describes the *origin of information* (Glean downstream, Clay, Gong transcript, user input). `actor` describes *who asserted the claim*. A single agent claim has `actor = 'agent:prepare_meeting:1.3'` and sources `[Glean { downstream: Salesforce }, Glean { downstream: Gong }]`. The two axes are orthogonal and remain so.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:220:- `propose_claim` and `commit_claim` service functions honoring the gate.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:245:- **Commit policy tuning requires real data.** The auto-commit trust threshold, corroboration threshold, tombstone window, and agent trust floor are all empirical. Expect a 60-day shadow-run post-substrate before the config is stable. During that period, agents run `strategy = "immediate"` to preserve current behavior; propose/commit is observational.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:253:- `DataSource` taxonomy unchanged. Actor and source are separate axes.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:262:### R1.1 State model fix — tombstones are a distinct state, not a committed-with-NULL
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:264:The original §2 and §5 read as if a tombstone is a committed claim with `claim_text = NULL` — but §7 also says retraction transitions a claim to `retracted` with the same semantics. Codex flagged this correctly: readers cannot tell whether a user removal is an asserted fact, a marker, or a hidden historical event.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:272:| `tombstoned` | Authoritative negative assertion — "this field is absent by intent." | NULL; `retraction_reason` required. | Included in default reads as a negative; blocks writers. |
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:274:| `withdrawn` | Explicitly retracted without supersede (source revocation, contradiction resolution, draft expiry). | As-written. | History only. |
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:276:`tombstoned` is its own state, not a variant of `committed`. It is a positive assertion of absence. Supersede/withdraw are for already-committed claims that leave the default read. This removes the internal contradiction.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:278:### R1.2 Supersede vs contradiction — clarified by source identity
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:285:The rule is: self-correction is supersede, cross-source disagreement is contradiction. Codify in the `commit_claim` service function: look up the latest committed claim at `field_path`; compare actors; route accordingly.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:287:### R1.3 Consolidate existing tombstone infrastructure, don't duplicate it
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:289:Reference pass found three pre-existing tombstone-like mechanisms that the original ADR ignored:
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:291:- `suppression_tombstones` table (migration `084_feedback_events.sql`) — generic item-level dismissals with `dismissed_at`, `expires_at`, `superseded_by_evidence_after`.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:293:- `account_stakeholder_roles.dismissed_at` column (migration `107_stakeholder_role_dismissals.sql`) — soft-delete with `data_source='user'` used by `intel_queue` to refuse re-insertion.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:295:**Consolidation timing — 2026-04-20 update:** Per plan-eng-review AskUserQuestion #1 and the brownfield-as-greenfield founder commitment (D1), consolidation ships in v1.4.0 as a destructive migration alongside the `intelligence_claims` table creation. Original R1.3 deferred consolidation to v1.4.1 out of migration caution; that deferral is retracted. Rationale: at AI velocity + minimal user base, keeping three parallel tombstone mechanisms alive through v1.4.0 creates interop cost that exceeds consolidation cost. The interop is what's expensive; one-table-to-rule-them-all is cleaner.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:299:- Write a `tombstoned` claim for every live row in `suppression_tombstones` (preserve `dismissed_at` as `created_at`; preserve `field_path` as `field_path`).
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:300:- Write a `tombstoned` claim for every `DismissedItem` found in `intelligence.json` blobs during the one-time backfill.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:301:- Write a `tombstoned` claim for every `account_stakeholder_roles` row with `dismissed_at IS NOT NULL` and `data_source='user'`.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:302:- Reads switch to `intelligence_claims` for all tombstone queries.
.docs/decisions/0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md:305:The ghost-resurrection fix becomes structural, not row-by-row. Consistency across stakeholder roles, dismissed intelligence items, and generic suppression tombstones is one code path, not three.
