.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:5:**Target:** v1.4.0 substrate (shared factor library, trust extractor) / v1.6.0 (health composer migration)
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:8:**Consumed by:** [DOS-5](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-5) Trust Compiler, [DOS-10](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-10) Freshness decay, [DOS-238](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-238) Factor library implementation
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:12:DailyOS has two composers that produce scores from entity signals: the **Trust Compiler** ([DOS-5](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-5)) which scores per-claim trustworthiness for the provenance envelope, and the **Account Health Composer** ([ADR-0097](0097-account-health-scoring-architecture.md)) which scores per-dimension health for briefings. As drafted, both reimplement the same primitive calculations — source reliability from Bayesian posteriors, freshness decay, corroboration boosts, contradiction penalties, user-feedback modulation. The math is identical; only the composition differs (weighted geometric mean for trust, lifecycle-weighted arithmetic for health).
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:14:Duplicating factor math is a correctness hazard. A fix to the freshness decay function in the trust path that does not also land in the health path produces silently divergent behavior — a claim that is "stale" for trust but "fresh" for health. Worse, the six health dimensions ([ADR-0097](0097-account-health-scoring-architecture.md)) already include freshness-like decay inside their composition, creating three implementations at risk of drifting.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:16:An adversarial review during v1.4.0 planning surfaced a second concern: "pure factors" cannot take an `EntityId` parameter and still be pure. A function that takes an ID implicitly reads the database, carries a hidden clock, or depends on global state — all of which break determinism under [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md)'s `Evaluate` mode and defeat [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md)'s fixture-replay guarantees.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:18:This ADR specifies the shared factor library, the per-composer extractor pattern, what stays inside each composer, and the confidence evidence structure that propagates to the UI. The goal is one source of truth per primitive, zero hidden state, and deterministic behavior under fixed inputs.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:22:### 1. Shared primitive factors
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:24:A single module, `src-tauri/src/scoring/factors.rs`, contains pure functions for cross-composer primitives. Every function in this module:
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:32:The canonical set is small and closed — new factors require an ADR amendment.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:35:// src-tauri/src/scoring/factors.rs
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:40:    source: &DataSource,
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:79:### 2. Per-surface extractors
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:81:Each composer has its own extractor that reads the database, injects the clock, and produces the typed factor inputs.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:83:**Trust extractor:** `src-tauri/src/scoring/extract/trust.rs`
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:86:pub async fn extract_trust_inputs(
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:94:    pub source: DataSource,
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:104:**Health extractor:** remains co-located with the health composer at `src-tauri/src/intelligence/health_scoring.rs`. Per-dimension extractors produce dimension-specific input structs that call the shared primitives where applicable.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:106:Extractors are where database access and clock injection happen. Factors never touch either. Extractors are tested with fixture-loaded databases; factors are tested with table-driven pure inputs.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:110:The six health dimensions from [ADR-0097](0097-account-health-scoring-architecture.md) — `meeting_cadence`, `email_engagement`, `stakeholder_coverage`, `champion_health`, `financial_proximity`, `signal_momentum` — are **not** promoted into `scoring::factors`. They are health-composer-internal compositions that may call shared primitives internally but express dimension-specific semantics (thresholds, lifecycle weights, per-dimension decay curves) that are not meaningful outside health.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:114:Health dimensions MAY invoke shared primitives like `freshness_weight` and `contradiction_penalty` from within their own composition. They may not maintain their own implementations of these — if a dimension needs a tweaked variant, the variant is either (a) a config parameter to the shared factor, or (b) a local helper in the dimension module that clearly documents why the shared factor didn't fit.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:118:Composers combine factors their own way. This is the point of the split.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:120:**Trust Compiler** ([DOS-5](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-5)): weighted geometric mean over six factors.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:123:trust_score = exp(Σ w_i × log(factor_i))
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:126:Weights, clamps, and bands live in `config/trust_compiler.toml`. The Trust Compiler reads primitives from `scoring::factors` and composes; it does not reimplement any primitive.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:137:Future composers (e.g., a v1.6.0+ "meeting priority" score, a v2.x "portfolio alert" score) will likewise have their own combinators and reuse `scoring::factors`.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:141:Each composer produces its own band taxonomy — trust uses `likely_current | use_with_caution | needs_verification`, health uses a dimension-specific color coding, future composers will define their own. Bands are not shared; forcing a shared band taxonomy would couple unrelated surfaces to a single scale.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:143:What **is** shared: a `ConfidenceEvidence` struct that every composer emits alongside its score, consumed uniformly by UI rendering.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:149:    pub factor_breakdown: Vec<FactorEvidence>,
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:153:pub struct FactorEvidence {
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:161:    StaleSource { source: DataSource, age_days: f64 },
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:167:UI surfaces render `ConfidenceEvidence` uniformly: a score, a band, an expandable breakdown, and a list of caveats. Bands differ per composer; the rendering shell is common. This is what [ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md) consumes.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:171:Factors are pure by construction. Extractors use the injected clock from [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) for any age computation. Under `ExecutionMode::Evaluate`:
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:173:- Extractors hit the fixture-loaded SQLite per [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md).
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:175:- Factor outputs are fully deterministic given fixture inputs.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:176:- Composer outputs are fully deterministic given factor outputs.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:178:This means both trust scores and health scores are replayable in the evaluation harness with no additional work beyond fixture creation. Regression tests for scoring changes become cheap.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:182:`src-tauri/tests/scoring/` holds the factor-level property tests:
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:184:- Each factor has a table-driven test with ≥10 boundary and typical cases.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:185:- Factor output ranges, monotonicity, and clamp behavior are property-tested against 10K random inputs.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:186:- Extractors have fixture-based tests that verify correct input assembly from representative DB states.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:195:├── [trust]
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:202:└── [factors]
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:213:- **One source of truth per primitive.** Fixing `freshness_weight` lands everywhere automatically. Drift between trust and health becomes impossible by construction.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:214:- **Determinism preserved.** Pure factors are replayable in the evaluation harness; no hidden state, no implicit DB reads.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:216:- **UI rendering uniform.** One `ConfidenceEvidence` shape means the product's explanation affordance works identically for trust and health without cross-coupling their semantics.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:217:- **Adversarial review finding addressed.** No factor takes `EntityId`; purity is structural, not conventional.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:218:- **Factor library is small and closed.** Six primitives, each well-bounded. Growth requires ADR amendment — forces us to be deliberate about what becomes universal.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:222:- **Extractors are real work per composer.** Each new composer pays a boilerplate cost to assemble inputs. Accepted — the cost is one-time per surface and keeps factor-level code clean.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:223:- **Refactoring health to use shared primitives is deferred.** The v1.4.0 cycle ships the library + trust extractor only. Health composer migration is v1.6.0 Hardening. During this window, health and trust compute freshness separately; drift is possible and must be caught by cross-composer property tests.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:225:- **Config namespace split requires migration.** Existing `config/trust_compiler.toml` references merge into `config/scoring.toml` with backward-compatible alias. Ships behind a feature flag.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:230:- `DataSource` taxonomy unchanged. [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md) continues to own source classification; this ADR consumes it.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:231:- `scoring::factors` is a new module but does not grow over time. A healthy sign.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:237:Post-draft adversarial review (codex) and codebase reference passes flagged that this ADR talks about creating a factor library as if one does not exist. In fact, factor-like code already exists in scattered places. Revision below.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:239:### R1.1 Existing factor code — this ADR is refactor, not greenfield
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:248:The original ADR treats `scoring::factors` as a net-new module. It is not. This ADR is a **consolidation refactor** that extracts the scattered primitives into a single pure-function module and makes the two composers (trust, health) consume it. Framing correction: amend §1's opening line to "A single module, `src-tauri/src/scoring/factors.rs`, **consolidates** pure functions for cross-composer primitives **currently scattered across `signals/bus.rs`, `signals/decay.rs`, `signals/fusion.rs`, and inline in `intelligence/health_scoring.rs`**."
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:252:- **Phase 0 (v1.4.0):** Audit the existing factor math; reconcile formula drift between health scoring and signal fusion; consolidate into `scoring::factors`; leave current call sites untouched but add the new module alongside.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:253:- **Phase 1 (v1.4.0):** Trust Compiler ([DOS-5](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-5)) consumes `scoring::factors` via the trust extractor.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:254:- **Phase 2 (v1.6.0):** Health scoring composer migrated to consume `scoring::factors`; `compute_account_health` made pure; DB-history writes extracted to a maintenance ability per [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md).
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:260:**Revised:** Phase 2 explicitly extracts the history-write side effect. The refactor:
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:275:Codex flagged: `UserFeedback { action, at: DateTime<Utc> }` smuggles time into a factor. Factors must not see clocks.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:277:**Revised:** the factor signature becomes:
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:284:    pub age_days: f64,             // Pre-computed by the extractor using injected clock
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:288:The extractor does the age computation. The factor never touches a clock. This extends the existing `FreshnessContext` pattern uniformly.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:292:Codex flagged: `meeting_relevance_weight(signal_type_affinity, meeting_context_match, attendee_overlap)` is specific to the Trust Compiler's meeting-prep path. It does not generalize. Promoting it to `scoring::factors` creates a factor that health scoring will never call and future composers will either ignore or misuse.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:294:**Revised:** `meeting_relevance_weight` moves from canonical factors to a trust-composer-local helper at `src-tauri/src/scoring/extract/trust.rs`. It is consumed only by Trust Compiler. Canonical factors drop to five: `source_reliability`, `freshness_weight`, `corroboration_weight`, `contradiction_penalty`, `user_feedback_weight`.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:296:### R1.5 `freshness_weight` takes DataSource — make dependency explicit
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:298:Codex flagged: `freshness_weight(source: &DataSource, ...)` bakes the source taxonomy into a generic scoring primitive. If that's the intent, it must be explicit about the dependency.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:300:**Revised:** `scoring::factors` depends on [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md) by design. The `DataSource` enum is the only external type factors take. The half-life table per source lives in `config/scoring.toml` under `[factors.freshness_half_lives]`. A future source taxonomy change requires a coordinated update to the config; that is a feature, not a bug.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:309:- Reload on config file change is **not** supported in v1.4.0; a restart applies new values. This is intentional: hot-reloading scoring config invalidates every cached trust score; better to require explicit restart.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:310:- Migration from today's hardcoded constants in `signals/bus.rs` and elsewhere happens in Phase 0: consolidate constants into `scoring::factors` defaults, emit config file with those defaults on first boot, delete the hardcoded constants.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:312:### R1.7 Inter-ADR fix — trust score home defined
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:314:Codex flagged: [ADR-0113](0113-human-and-agent-analysis-as-first-class-claim-sources.md) says trust depends on accepted/corroborated/retracted outcomes, but this ADR doesn't define where trust score **lives** relative to the canonical scoring stack.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:316:**Revised:** trust score lives on the claim row (`intelligence_claims.trust_score` per [DOS-7](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-7)), computed by the Trust Compiler ([DOS-5](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-5)) which is the single composer that reads `scoring::factors` for trust purposes. The `corroboration_weight` factor reads from `claim_corroborations` (ADR-0113 R1.6); `user_feedback_weight` reads from `user_feedback_signals` ([DOS-8](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-8)); `contradiction_penalty` reads from `claim_contradictions` (ADR-0113 §7). All three inputs are claim-adjacent; the compiler stitches them in the trust extractor.
.docs/decisions/0114-scoring-unification.md:320:- Phase 0 (factor consolidation + existing code refactor).
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:7:**Supersedes:** The narrow `DataSource` enums implicit in [ADR-0098](0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md) and [ADR-0100](0100-glean-first-intelligence-architecture.md); this ADR defines the authoritative taxonomy.  
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:8:**Required by:** [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §4, [ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md)
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:16:- [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §4 references `DataSource` in provenance attribution, including Salesforce and others not in ADR-0098's enum.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:18:The field naming is also inconsistent: [ADR-0080](0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md) uses `source`; [ADR-0098](0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md) and [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) use `data_source`. Lifecycle behavior per source (purge vs. flag-for-re-enrichment) is also not consistent: [ADR-0098](0098-data-governance-source-aware-lifecycle.md) says AI-generated intelligence is flagged for re-enrichment rather than purged, but downstream consumers (provenance, maintenance audit) had been assumed to purge.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:20:This ADR defines the authoritative `DataSource` enum, the `SourceIdentifier` extension pattern, the canonical field name, and the per-source lifecycle rules that every provenance-consuming surface honors.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:24:### 1. Authoritative `DataSource` Enum
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:27:pub enum DataSource {
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:58:**Glean is modeled as an aggregator, not as a single source.** When an ability attributes `DataSource::Glean { downstream: Salesforce }`, it captures both the retrieval path (Glean) and the originating source (Salesforce). This preserves ADR-0100's Glean-first posture without erasing downstream lineage needed for trust and lifecycle decisions.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:72:`SourceIdentifier` from [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §4 gains variants to match the expanded `DataSource` enum:
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:91:    // Direct provider invocations
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:92:    ProviderCompletion { completion_id: String, provider: ProviderKind },
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:109:**Glean-sourced opaque data attribution.** When Glean returns an assessment derived from content DailyOS has no local visibility into (e.g., a Zendesk ticket the user's Glean account can read but DailyOS cannot), the ability attributes `DataSource::Glean { downstream: Zendesk }` with `SourceIdentifier::OpaqueGleanSource { downstream: Zendesk, opaque_ref, ... }`. Lifecycle of opaque sources is delegated to the user's Glean policy — DailyOS cannot purge data it does not hold, but it can mask or invalidate derived artifacts when the Glean connection is revoked.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:113:Every `DataSource` variant has a scoring class that determines how it contributes to numeric scoring:
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:115:| DataSource | ScoringClass | Reasoning |
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:131:The `scoring_class` field on `SourceAttribution` ([ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §4) is derived from this table, not author-set. Consumers that aggregate sources for scoring filter by class to avoid conflating Slack chatter with Salesforce ARR.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:156:| DataSource | LifecycleBehavior |
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:166:**Resolution of the ADR-0098/ADR-0105 conflict.** ADR-0098 is right: AI-synthesized output is flagged, not purged, because the underlying source revocation removes the input data but the synthesis itself may remain useful as an annotation awaiting re-generation. [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §8 is amended: "source revocation triggers a masking pass" applies only to sources whose `LifecycleBehavior` is `Mask`; for `Purge`, the records are deleted; for `FlagForReEnrichment`, the records remain with a flag. [ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md) specifies the masking and flag-rendering shapes surfaces see.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:170:Every stored field that is itself LLM-synthesized carries a `SynthesisMarker` (per [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §4) so the trust model's `contains_stored_synthesis` check works. This ADR specifies the marker storage:
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:173:- Migration-time backfill: existing stored AI output is tagged retroactively based on the ability that produced it (best-effort; some historical data may not be attributable and is marked `synthesized_fields: ["*unknown*"]` to err on the side of `Untrusted`).
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:174:- Reading a synthesized field populates `SourceAttribution.synthesis_marker` so the trust builder sees it.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:182:1. **One authoritative `DataSource` enum.** No drift between ADRs; every consumer uses the same values.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:188:7. **Stored AI output is identifiable.** Synthesis markers close the trust-laundering hole from [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §3.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:192:1. **Expanded enum is more surface area.** Every consumer that switches on `DataSource` must handle more variants.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:194:3. **Synthesis marker backfill imperfect.** Historical data may be tagged conservatively as `*unknown*`, slightly inflating `Untrusted` trust assessments until data churns.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:209:- [ADR-0105: Provenance as First-Class Output](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) — §4 `SourceAttribution` uses this ADR's `DataSource` and `SourceIdentifier`.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:210:- [ADR-0108: Provenance Rendering and Privacy](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md) — Renders masking behavior and flag-for-re-enrichment status.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:215:## Amendment — 2026-04-24 PM — `LegacyUnattributed` `DataSource` variant
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:219:Codex round 2 finding 13 surfaced that the v1.4.0 spine plan (DOS-299) needed to preserve item-level confidence semantics for legacy items without `ItemSource` (the current `HasSource::effective_confidence` defaults missing source to `0.5` per `intelligence/io.rs:1185-1189`). My earlier writeup proposed registering this in the `CLAIM_TYPE_REGISTRY` (per ADR-0125). That was wrong — it's a `DataSource` taxonomy concern, not a claim type. ADR-0107 owns `DataSource`; this amendment adds the variant.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:223:Add to the `DataSource` enum:
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:226:pub enum DataSource {
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:232:    LegacyUnattributed,
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:241:- **Backfill-only:** new code MUST NOT write `LegacyUnattributed` claims. Production migration is the only legitimate writer. CI lint enforces no `DataSource::LegacyUnattributed` outside `services/migrations/` paths.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:245:- v1.4.0 spine: backfill writes `LegacyUnattributed` claims for legacy items without `ItemSource`. Trust band parity preserved per DOS-299 acceptance criterion.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:246:- v1.4.1+: enrichment paths supersede `LegacyUnattributed` claims with typed-source claims as they re-enrich entities. Number of `LegacyUnattributed` claims decreases monotonically.
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:247:- v1.5.x: when `LegacyUnattributed` claim count drops below 5% of total active claims, schedule retirement migration (drop the variant, force any remaining to be re-enriched or flagged).
.docs/decisions/0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md:251:- DOS-299 — backfill writes `LegacyUnattributed` for legacy items; preserves trust band parity.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:6:**Extends:** [ADR-0091](0091-intelligence-provider-abstraction.md), [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md)  
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:8:**Related:** [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md) (consumer of fingerprints)
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:12:[ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §1 declares that every ability that invokes the intelligence provider records a `PromptFingerprint` in its provenance envelope. [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md) (forthcoming) uses the fingerprint to distinguish prompt regressions from input regressions during evaluation. [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) §6 requires replay providers to produce deterministic completions in `Evaluate` mode.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:14:[ADR-0091](0091-intelligence-provider-abstraction.md) defines `IntelligenceProvider::complete(prompt, tier) -> String`. This signature does not expose the metadata — provider name, model, token counts, seed, prompt template version — that `PromptFingerprint` requires. ADR-0091 was scoped to the intel queue; ADR-0102 expanded provider use to all Transform abilities. This ADR formally amends ADR-0091's trait to expose fingerprint-capable metadata and specifies the fingerprint shape and canonicalization rules.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:18:### 1. The `PromptFingerprint` Shape
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:21:pub struct PromptFingerprint {
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:22:    pub provider: ProviderKind,            // Enum: ClaudeCode | Ollama | OpenAI | Other(&'static str)
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:26:    pub canonical_prompt_hash: Hash,       // SHA256 of canonicalized template + inputs
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:32:    pub provider_completion_id: Option<String>, // When the provider supplies a stable ID
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:36:**ModelName is a newtype, not `&'static str`.** Models are configurable at runtime per [ADR-0091](0091-intelligence-provider-abstraction.md); a static string would prevent user-selected models from being captured.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:38:**`provider_completion_id` is optional.** Claude Code over PTY and local Ollama may not expose a stable completion ID; OpenAI and hosted Anthropic APIs do. Consumers MUST NOT depend on it being present.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:40:### 2. Canonicalization Rules for `canonical_prompt_hash`
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:46:3. The hash input is the concatenation of: prompt template ID, prompt template version, canonicalized template bytes, canonical JSON of input values, provider kind, model name, temperature bytes (fixed-width `f32` big-endian), top_p (if set), seed (if set).
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:56:    pub fingerprint_metadata: FingerprintMetadata,
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:60:    pub provider: ProviderKind,
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:67:    pub provider_completion_id: Option<String>,
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:73:    fn provider_kind(&self) -> ProviderKind;
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:78:`PromptInput` carries the template ID, version, and pre-materialized input values so the provider can compute `tokens_input` accurately. Provider implementations produce `FingerprintMetadata` from their actual invocation; abilities combine it with the prompt-template hash to produce the full `PromptFingerprint` stored in provenance.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:80:**Backward compatibility with ADR-0091.** Existing callers that receive only the text can do so via a thin helper `provider.complete(prompt, tier).await?.text`. The text field is unchanged. The added metadata is additive.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:84:[ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) §6 requires replay providers in `Evaluate` mode. This ADR specifies:
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:86:**Replay is keyed by `canonical_prompt_hash`, not by seed.** A replay provider's fixture maps `canonical_prompt_hash → Completion`. Looking up a completion by hash gives the fixture's recorded response. If the hash is missing from the fixture, the replay provider returns `Error::FixtureMissingCompletion { hash }`.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:88:**Seed is for live generation determinism, not replay.** When `Evaluate` mode drives a live provider (e.g., running a regression test against a real model to capture a new fixture), `seed` is mandatory; the provider passes it through to the model API. When `Evaluate` mode drives a replay provider, `seed` is retained in the fingerprint for audit but does not drive the replay.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:90:**Fixture construction.** Running an ability in `Live` mode with a tracing wrapper records every provider invocation (prompt template ID, inputs, metadata, completion). The captured sequence is the replay fixture for future `Evaluate` runs. [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md) specifies fixture storage, anonymization, and refresh cadence.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:104:Prompt changes are independent of ability schema versions ([ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §8). A prompt bump produces a new fingerprint without bumping the ability version. `prompt_template_version` in the fingerprint lets consumers distinguish prompt-driven output changes from schema-driven ones.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:108:[ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md) uses fingerprints for regression classification:
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:110:- Same inputs, same `prompt_template_version`, different outputs → provider drift or fixture inconsistency
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:112:- Different inputs, same fingerprint otherwise → real input change
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:113:- Different `canonical_prompt_hash`, same template version → canonicalization bug (template edit that didn't bump version, or input-materialization drift)
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:119:1. **Provider metadata captured consistently.** Every LLM invocation records provider, model, temperature, seed, tokens, and completion ID where available.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:120:2. **Evaluation distinguishes prompt regressions from input regressions.** The fingerprint classification in §6 is structurally sound.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:123:5. **Canonicalization prevents spurious fingerprint churn.** Whitespace and JSON-key reordering no longer change the hash.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:128:2. **Every provider implementation must supply `FingerprintMetadata`.** Claude Code PTY and Ollama implementations need to extract model name, temperature, tokens from their response flows. Non-trivial for PTY-based providers that historically returned only text.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:134:1. **Provider metadata incompleteness.** A PTY provider cannot extract token counts reliably. Mitigation: metadata fields are `Option<u32>`. Consumers handle missing values.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:138:5. **Replay fixture staleness.** Live provider behavior drifts from fixture. Mitigation: fixtures are refreshed on a declared cadence. Staleness is flagged at evaluation time when a replay produces unexpected outputs vs. a live spot-check.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:142:- [ADR-0091: IntelligenceProvider Abstraction](0091-intelligence-provider-abstraction.md) — Amended by this ADR: `complete()` returns `Completion` instead of `String`.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:144:- [ADR-0104: ExecutionMode and Mode-Aware Services](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) — §6 requires determinism in `Evaluate` mode, satisfied by replay keyed by `canonical_prompt_hash`.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:145:- [ADR-0105: Provenance as First-Class Output](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) — §1 `PromptFingerprint` shape referenced; this ADR provides the definition.
.docs/decisions/0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md:146:- **ADR-0110 (forthcoming): Evaluation Harness for Abilities** — Consumes fingerprints for regression classification.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:6:**Extends:** [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md), [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md)  
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:11:[ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) defines the `Provenance` envelope and requires surfaces to render it. It defers three concerns to this ADR:
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:14:2. **Privacy and safety** — what gets masked, redacted, or filtered when rendered to different actors or published externally.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:15:3. **The `ProvenanceMasked` shape** referenced by [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §8 and used when sources are revoked.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:17:[ADR-0094](0094-audit-log-and-enterprise-observability.md) governs the append-only JSONL security audit with strict PII hygiene. [ADR-0093](0093-prompt-injection-hardening.md) established that LLM outputs can contain adversarial content. This ADR integrates both: provenance must be renderable to users and agents without leaking internal graph structure or PII, and sanitization of LLM-generated `explanation` text must prevent re-injection.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:26:- Expanding the affordance shows: ability name, produced_at timestamp (relative + absolute), number of sources, trust assessment (Trusted / Untrusted with reason), composition depth summary.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:27:- A secondary "Details" expansion shows field-level attributions: for each significant output field, the sources that contributed, their `data_source` (using human-readable names via [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md)'s `DataSource::display_name()`), and any `confidence` score.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:28:- A third-level "Full provenance" expansion shows the JSON envelope for power users and debugging.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:33:- Every tool response includes provenance by default. The `Provenance` envelope serializes as a structured field alongside the domain output.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:34:- Actor-filtered contents: the MCP wrapper strips content from provenance that is not permitted for `Actor::Agent`:
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:36:  - Child provenance trees are collapsed to `ChildElided { ability_name, data_sources_summary }` beyond depth 2.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:37:  - Prompt fingerprints include `provider`, `model`, `prompt_template_id`, `prompt_template_version`, but NOT `canonical_prompt_hash` (internal) or `seed`.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:39:- An MCP tool `get_provenance(invocation_id)` provides a fuller view for authorized agents if needed; default response is summary-sized.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:43:- Publications carry a provenance footnote listing the top-level sources by `data_source` class (e.g., "Based on Salesforce, recent meetings, and team activity") — **no source identifiers, no entity names, no attribution text**.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:44:- Expandable "Details" block is available in the publication renderer but only if the publishing user confirms at publish time that the detail level is appropriate for the P2 audience. This is an explicit confirmation in the publish dialog, not a default.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:45:- Synthesis markers render as "AI-generated summary" prefix on the publication body.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:46:- Trust classification does not render to external readers; it is internal operational metadata.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:51:- Application logs reference `invocation_id` and do not include provenance content; to retrieve a run's provenance, query the audit table.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:56:A universal renderer transforms `Provenance` into an actor-appropriate view:
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:59:pub fn render_provenance_for(prov: &Provenance, actor: Actor, surface: Surface) -> RenderedProvenance { ... }
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:64:    McpToolDetail,    // Actor-filtered, full-detail (invoked via get_provenance)
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:70:The renderer consults `actor` and `surface` to determine what to include. A user rendering provenance in the app sees the full graph; an agent accessing the same ability's output via MCP sees a filtered version; a P2 publication sees source classes only.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:72:**Agents never see:** internal signal IDs, entity IDs (unless explicitly part of the response schema), raw `prompt_template` text, internal `canonical_prompt_hash`, internal entity version watermarks. These are operational internals; surfacing them to agents gives attackers a map of the user's graph.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:76:LLM-generated `explanation` text is untrusted per [ADR-0093](0093-prompt-injection-hardening.md). It can contain prompt injection attempts, PII from other sources, or links to attacker-controlled content. Every explanation is passed through a sanitizer before rendering:
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:78:1. **HTML entities encoded.** Any `<`, `>`, `&`, `"`, `'` is entity-escaped. No raw HTML or Markdown links rendered from explanations.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:80:3. **URL stripping.** Anything resembling a URL (regex-based) is removed and replaced with `[url removed]`. If the explanation wants to cite a URL, it must come through a typed `SourceAttribution` with a URL field, not through free text.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:82:5. **No executable content.** Regardless of surface, explanations are never passed to a markdown renderer, a JS `eval` context, or any other dynamic evaluator.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:84:The sanitizer is shared across all surfaces; the rendering layer calls it once per `FieldAttribution` before composing the rendered output.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:88:When a source is revoked and its associated provenance records are masked per [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md) §5:
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:97:    pub sources_masked: Vec<DataSource>,     // Which sources triggered the masking
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:101:    SourceRevoked { data_source: DataSource },
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:110:**Masking is irreversible.** Once a provenance is masked, the original content is discarded. Re-running the ability after the source is re-connected produces a new invocation with its own provenance. This is the cost of the mask — diagnosis of past runs becomes unavailable for revoked sources, but user privacy is protected.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:114:[ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §9 specifies storage size budgets. Rendering has its own:
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:116:- **Tauri app initial render:** top-level summary ≤ 2KB. Expansion fetches additional detail on demand.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:117:- **MCP default response:** provenance payload ≤ 10KB. Full detail available via `get_provenance(invocation_id)`.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:120:When rendering exceeds a budget, the renderer truncates deepest-first (children elided first, sources last) and surfaces a `ProvenanceWarning::TruncatedForRender { surface, budget_bytes, original_bytes }`.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:124:[ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §7 declared "every surface that displays ability output MUST be capable of rendering provenance." This ADR narrows the "MUST" to outputs where provenance rendering is meaningful:
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:137:- Settings list views (each row can show a "source count" indicator; full provenance on tap)
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:139:The test: if the output is a *composed* or *synthesized* artifact the user will reason about, render provenance. If it is a *presentation affordance* showing a value, a count, or a name, the rendering is optional (but the provenance still exists in storage and can be queried).
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:145:1. **Rendering rules are surface-specific.** The Tauri app gets rich detail; MCP gets actor-filtered; P2 gets heavy redaction; logs get operational metadata. No one-size-fits-all.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:147:3. **LLM-generated explanations are sanitized.** Prompt injection through `explanation` fields is defanged before rendering.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:149:5. **Size budgets bounded per surface.** Rendering cost is predictable; runaway provenance payloads are truncated with an explicit warning.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:150:6. **Rendering-required boundary is clear.** Not every UI element bears the "MUST render" burden; composed and synthesized artifacts do.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:154:1. **Renderer complexity grows.** A shared `render_provenance_for(prov, actor, surface)` function with branching logic per surface and actor is non-trivial. Worth it.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:156:3. **MCP `get_provenance(invocation_id)` tool adds surface.** Agents can request detail they did not receive by default.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:162:2. **Renderer drift across surfaces.** The Tauri app diverges from MCP in how it interprets provenance. Mitigation: `render_provenance_for` is the single entry point; surfaces cannot bypass it.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:163:3. **Masked provenance hides legitimate debugging.** User reports data issue; the relevant maintenance audit is masked. Mitigation: masking is irreversible as a matter of privacy. Live re-run reproduces the data issue with fresh provenance.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:164:4. **P2 publication leaks through "Details" toggle.** User opts in to full detail and leaks internal IDs. Mitigation: publication "Details" renders through the same actor filter as MCP when the P2 audience includes non-team members; team-internal publishes get full detail.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:165:5. **Tauri `canonical_prompt_hash` leakage in DevTools.** Power users inspecting the Tauri bridge can see internal details. Mitigation: accepted — the Tauri app runs as the user's process; there is no threat model where the user is attacking themselves.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:169:- [ADR-0105: Provenance as First-Class Output](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) — Defines the envelope; this ADR specifies rendering.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:170:- [ADR-0107: Source Taxonomy Alignment](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md) — Specifies `DataSource::display_name()` and `ScoringClass`; rendering uses both.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:171:- [ADR-0094: Audit Log and Enterprise Observability](0094-audit-log-and-enterprise-observability.md) — Security audit remains separate from provenance rendering.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:172:- [ADR-0093: Prompt Injection Hardening](0093-prompt-injection-hardening.md) — Explanation sanitizer aligns with ADR-0093's untrusted-content rules.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:174:- **ADR-0111 (forthcoming): Surface-Independent Ability Invocation** — Consumes the per-surface rendering rules specified here.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:178:## Amendment — 2026-04-20 — Enforce 64 KB provenance size cap
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:182:§6 originally introduced size budgets as a soft guideline. In practice a deeply-composed ability (meeting prep composing context which composes claims which compose trajectories) can construct a provenance envelope of several hundred KB. Tauri IPC has serialization limits; this will hit them. Persona review flagged: named limits without enforcement become silent bloat.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:184:**Hard cap: 64 KB serialized provenance per ability output.**
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:186:- The cap applies to the full `Provenance` envelope after JSON serialization, before any surface rendering.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:192:1. **Provenance summarization** — collapse deep composition trees into summary nodes marked `ProvenanceWarning::DepthElided`. [ADR-0105](0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md) §1 already declares this warning class; this amendment activates it.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:193:2. **Shallower composition** — refactor the ability to compose fewer children or to use an alternative data source.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:196:**Starting value of 64 KB is conservative.** Measure during the first end-to-end slice (strategy doc § Path forward action 1). If real meeting-prep provenance is larger than 64 KB on representative entities, raise the cap via amendment with the measurement as justification. Do not raise silently.
.docs/decisions/0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md:198:**Rendering budget (original §6) vs serialization budget (this amendment):** different concerns. The rendering budget limits what the renderer emits for a given surface; this cap limits what the envelope itself can carry regardless of surface. Both exist independently; rendering truncation does not fix a too-large envelope because the envelope is already over-cap before the renderer sees it.
.docs/decisions/0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md:8:**Required by:** [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md) §8 (audit), [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §10 (trust boundary)  
.docs/decisions/0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md:9:**Companions:** [ADR-0106](0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md) (prompt fingerprint details), [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md) (`DataSource` taxonomy), [ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md) (surface rendering and safety)
.docs/decisions/0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md:13:[ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §9 Rule 5 declares that every ability output carries provenance via `AbilityOutput<T>`. §10 declares Transform outputs are untrusted for mutation authorization. §11.3 declares composition produces nested provenance trees. [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md) §8 requires maintenance audit records to carry provenance. All of these contracts depend on one shape: the `Provenance` envelope. ADR-0102 references it but does not specify it.
.docs/decisions/0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md:15:This ADR defines the envelope shape, trust classification, and composition semantics. It deliberately does not define: prompt fingerprinting (see [ADR-0106](0106-prompt-fingerprinting-and-provider-interface.md)), the authoritative `DataSource` taxonomy (see [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md)), or per-surface rendering rules (see [ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md)). Splitting these concerns keeps each ADR implementable atomically and testable in isolation.
.docs/decisions/0105-provenance-as-first-class-output.md:19:Every ability output carries a `Provenance` envelope via the `AbilityOutput<T>` wrapper from [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §6. The envelope captures identity, temporal context, trust classification, source attribution, composition tree, and field-level attribution.
